                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e2150770-8a04-3604-b506-fcf20e338739",
                                        "entry_id": "d7a65576-5dca-3e32-a746-a993c63fb302",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of, pertaining to, or influenced by political economy."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0f66c4cd-ed73-4c16-9d67-4d4943425bcd",
                                                "sense_id": "e2150770-8a04-3604-b506-fcf20e338739",
                                                "text": "But, as Marx was later to remark, “the anatomy of civil society is political economy,” and thus a radical critique of human alienation required the development of a political-economic critique as well."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "aefc4b3d-2dd1-45e5-8ddf-b43b6ed54bd7",
                                                "sense_id": "e2150770-8a04-3604-b506-fcf20e338739",
                                                "text": "Political economy must begin with a recognition of the inherently conflictual nature of international relations. The nation is, as early mercantilists held, the key unit of political-economic analysis."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c05ac068-e470-4f6a-b6d0-a19b82b6784b",
                                                "sense_id": "e2150770-8a04-3604-b506-fcf20e338739",
                                                "text": "[…] it is necessary for political economists who wish to be similarly critical to be self-conscious of their method of theorizing as much as they are self-conscious of their political-economic theory and its concepts so that those concepts, and even the theory itself, do not become static but instead remain perpetually critical."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
